text:
Free radicals exhibit diverse thermodynamic properties, ranging from highly oxidizing to highly reducing. These properties can be used to predict a hierarchy, or pecking order, for free radical reactions. Using one-electron reduction potentials, the predicted hierarchy aligns with experimentally observed free radical electron transfer reactions. These potentials are also supported by data indicating that vitamin E, the primary lipid-soluble small molecule antioxidant, and vitamin C, the terminal water-soluble small molecule antioxidant, cooperate to protect lipids and lipid structures against peroxidation. Although vitamin E is located in membranes and vitamin C is located in aqueous phases, vitamin C can recycle vitamin E by repairing the tocopheroxyl (chromanoxyl) radical of vitamin E, allowing vitamin E to function again as a free radical chain-breaking antioxidant. This review covers:

 (i) The thermodynamics of free radical reactions relevant to the health sciences; 
(ii) The fundamental thermodynamic and kinetic properties associated with chain-breaking antioxidants; 
(iii) The unique interfacial nature of the apparent reaction of the tocopherol free radical (vitamin E radical) and vitamin C; and (iv) Presents a hierarchy, or pecking order, for free radical electron (hydrogen atom) transfer reactions.
